ame '*commonsense*' -o -iname '*hellaswag*' -o -iname '*winogrande*' -o -iname '*lm_eval*' \\\\) 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo '--- wheels/cache ---'; ls /var/cache/pip /root/.cache 2>&1; find / -maxdepth 3 -name '*.whl' 2>/dev/null | head; echo '--- network test ---'; timeout 5 python3 -c \\\"import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org', timeout=3)\\\" 2>&1 | tail -1\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
{"id": "6abad57f-f237-4520-9ac2-ddf8a9e22d21", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T13:55:45.756903", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "e155e372-3328-42ca-a224-e9c4a57792cd", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bd5d43776f72440f", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"find / -maxdepth 4 \\\\( -iname '*fusellm*' -o -iname '*llama*' -o -iname '*commonsense*' -o -iname '*hellaswag*' -o -iname '*winogrande*' -o -iname '*lm_eval*' \\\\) 2>/dev/null | head -20; echo '--- wheels/cache ---'; ls /var/cache/pip /root/.cache 2>&1; find / -maxdepth 3 -name '*.whl' 2>/dev/null | head; echo '--- network test ---'; timeout 5 python3 -c \\\"import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('https://pypi.org', timeout=3)\\\" 2>&1 | tail -1\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.366, \"output\": \"--- wheels/cache ---\\nls: cannot access '/var/cache/pip': No such file or directory\\nls: cannot access '/root/.cache': No such file or directory\\n--- network test ---\\nurllib.error.URLError: <urlopen error [Errno -3] Temporary failure in name resolution>\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "e155e372-3328-42ca-a224-e9c4a57792cd", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "f11f0e0c-9f21-4f99-b933-84ae3d00e6df", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T13:57:42.110596", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "6abad57f-f237-4520-9ac2-ddf8a9e22d21", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Environment inspection is complete. I have a clear picture of what exists and what's absent, so I'll now propose the high-level approach."}], "reasoning_content": "Confirmed: the environment is completely bare. No FuseLLM code, no Llama-2 weights, no eval harness, no datasets, no ML libraries, no network, no GPU. Python 3.14, 224 CPUs, roughly 2TB RAM.\n\nSo the design-only experiment has to be executable somewhere else (in the researcher's actual compute environment), or the design has to explicitly state what assets would be required. The task documents say \"design-only task; repository and compute are not required.\"\n\nNext, I should recall my knowledge of the FuseLLM paper (FuseLLM: Fusing Llama and Mistral for Commonsense Reasoning, ICLR 2024, arXiv:2401.13359). The researcher's topics: iclr2024/18013/1:setup_2 and setup_9 — probably referring to sections of the paper's experimental setup.\n\nKey facts from the FuseLLM paper (from my knowledge):\n- FuseLLM fuses the weights of Llama-2-7b-chat and Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 by linear interpolation with a fusion ratio α ∈ {0, 0.1, ..., 1.0}: θ_fused = α·θ_Llama + (1−α)·θ_Mistral. Actually, the paper says \"FuseLLM fuses the weights of open-source LLMs Llama-2-7b-chat and Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 using a fusion ratio of 0.5.\"\n- The FuseLLM suite includes 8 commonsense reasoning datasets: BoolQ, PIQA, SIQA, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, OpenBookQA. Plus CommonsenseQA. Actually, the \"CommenSense (CS)\" benchmark set in the FuseLLM paper (and the Llama-2 paper): BoolQ, PIQA, SIQA, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, ARC-e, ARC-c, OBQA — the \"CS\" suite from Llama 2. The FuseLLM paper evaluates on: CommonsenseQA, HellaSwag, ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, BoolQ, OpenBookQA, PIQA, SIQA, WinoGrande.\n- Evaluation: zero-shot, via the OpenLLM leaderboard style / lm-evaluation-harness, accuracy metric. The FuseLLM paper uses the Open LLM Leaderboard evaluation harness (lm-eval-harness, few-shot 0? Actually the OpenLLM leaderboard uses few-shot settings: ARC 25-shot, etc.). But the question says zero-shot. Paper: \"We evaluate FuseLLM on nine NLP benchmarks… zero-shot\"? Let me recall. The FuseLLM paper (Chu et al. 2024, \"FuseLLM: Fusing Llama and Mistral for Commonsense Reasoning\") reports accuracy on CommonsenseQA, HellaSwag, ARC-easy, ARC-challenge, BoolQ, OpenBookQA, PIQA, SIQA, WinoGrande, using the OpenLLM Leaderboard evaluation harness? Hmm. Actually I recall they used HuggingFace's Open LLM Leaderboard tools with default (few-shot) settings, and reported an average improvement of ~4.4% over the best baseline. But the question explicitly says \"zero-shot\".\n\nThe topics iclr2024/18013/1:setup_2 and setup_9 are probably specific setup claims in the paper. I can't retrieve them (no network). I should flag the ambiguity and list it as an assumption to verify with the researcher.\n\nEvaluation protocol for multiple-choice commonsense tasks: lm-eval loglikelihood (\"acc\" over continuation likelihoods, LAMBADA-style multiple-choice normalization; the harness default for MMLU-style is unnormalized; for PIQA etc. the OpenLLM leaderboard uses acc and acc_norm). Generation tasks like CommonsenseQA can use exact-match on the generated letter/answer. WinoGrande is multiple-choice loglikelihood. BoolQ is a yes/no loglikelihood.\n\nFusion variants: fusion ratio α. Paper: FuseLLM-7b uses α=0.5; ablations over α ∈ {0.1..0.9}; Mistral-fused variants? They also compare against GPT-3.5, Vicuna, and other 7B models. The core comparison for this question is: fused checkpoint (α=0.5) vs Llama-2-7b-chat baseline. Should include Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 as a control, and sweep α to see whether the effect is due to fusion itself rather than one side.\n\nFeasibility in this environment: no GPU, no network, no models — can't run here. A 7B bf16 model on 224 CPUs, 2TB RAM is feasible with CPU inference (e.g., llama.cpp GGUF quantization or accelerate CPU), but slow; a rough estimate: CPU bf16 forward on 2 cores is… but 224 cores might help with batching; still, loglikelihood evaluation on HellaSwag (70k continuation scoring) is heavy. Note: the design is a deliverable; track.md says compute is not required.\n\nStatistics: average accuracy across 8/9 tasks; paired comparisons at the item level (bootstrap / McNemar for binary task outcomes); the single deterministic decoding doesn't involve sampling variation, but quantization/prompt variance exists. Use bf16 to avoid quantization confounds.\n\nAlright, I'll submit stage=plan together with investigation findings, proposed approach, and assumptions. Keep it to a plan, not a full design. Use end_researcher_turn.\n\nLet me structure this. Also, should I ask the researcher for clarification on the setup topics? I can list open questions. Question budget 6, but end_researcher_turn is the vehicle for this.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## 1. Inspection findings (verified, no fabrication)\n\n**Repository `/workspace`:** effectively empty — contains only `task.json` (the task spec itself). No FuseLLM code, no Llama-2/Mistral configs or weights, no lm-evaluation-harness / OpenAI-eval / Open-LLM-Leaderboard code, no commonsense datasets.\n\n**Search of the filesystem** for `*fusellm*`, `*llama*`, `*commonsense*`, `*hellaswag*`, `*winogrande*`, `*lm_eval*`, and stray `.whl` files: **nothing found**. No `/data`, `/models`, `/opt`, no HF cache.\n\n**Software:** Python 3.14.4 with system packages only; **no** torch, transformers, datasets, accelerate, lm-eval, numpy, scipy, pandas. **Network confirmed disabled** (DNS resolution failure to pypi.org). **No GPU** (nvidia-smi fails). **Available compute:** 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM (1.9 TB ava