{"id": "c91db19c-a3f2-4285-bde8-59a9c5bf3df6", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T13:58:41.939201", "source": "user", "parent_id": "393e9215-c7fc-4cd3-bc66-a72044e003b1", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved. Your inspection is consistent with the stated design-only brief, and the proposed structure (fused-vs-parent comparison, matched zero-shot protocol, paired item-level inference, precision controls) is the right shape. Produce the full design document now. Before you do, here are my resolutions on your open questions — treat these as my directed design choices (my proposals, not facts from the paper), and record them as stated assumptions in the doc:\\n\\n1. CS suite membership: use the Llama-2-lineage 8-task commonsense (CS) set as the PRIMARY endpoint (BoolQ, PIQA, SIQA, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, ARC-E, ARC-C, OBQA), because the question phrases it as \\\"standard benchmarks CommenSense(CS)\\\". Include CommonsenseQA as a SECONDARY supplementary task (report separately; do not mix into the primary average) so the design covers both readings without contaminating the headline metric.\\n2. Definition of \\\"outperform\\\": primary = difference in macro-average accuracy across the 8 CS tasks, FuseLLM(α=0.5) minus Llama-2-7b-chat, under matched zero-shot conditions, judged by item-level paired bootstrap 95% CI plus a per-task sign/consistency check. Per-task win/loss is a secondary descriptor, not a separate gate.\\n3. Checkpoints: use the official released FuseLLM-7B checkpoint as the primary fused condition; local re-fusion by linear weight interpolation at the same α as a REPRODUCIBILITY CONTROL (agreement check, not the headline number). Keep the α-sweep as a diagnostic/secondary analysis only.\\n4. Parent control: keep Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 evaluated under the identical protocol as a control condition, since it is essential for attributing the delta to fusion rather than to the stronger parent.\\n5. Prompting: use each model's native chat template with harness-default zero-shot task templates, identical across conditions; report both acc and acc_norm for MC tasks and state explicitly which is primary (acc_norm for length-biased tasks such as HellaSwag/OBQA, with the choice justified rather than inherited from the paper's leaderboard settings).\\n6. Precision: bf16 for the primary analysis; add an explicit quantization-confound control (e.g., one task at two precisions) so the design shows how a quantization artifact would be detected. No quantized results in the headline comparison.\\n7. Paper numbers: treat published FuseLLM/Llama-2 accuracies strictly as reference context; the decision rule is internal, matched-condition comparison. Note in the doc that the paper's leaderboard-style settings may not be zero-shot, so absolute values are expected to differ.\\n\\nDeliverable now: the final experiment design document with (a) rationale tying each condition/control to the hypothesis, (b) full protocol (data/task configs, models, fusion definition and α handling, scoring functions, decoding settings, hardware/compute budget estimate), (c) controls list (parent control, re-fusion control, precision control, tokenizer/architecture compatibility pre-check, and a negative control such as α=0/α=1 identity checks), (d) analysis plan with pre-registered metrics, paired-bootstrap and sign-test procedures, multiple-comparison handling for the per-task table, and the decision rule, (e) feasibility section stating explicitly what cannot be run in this sandbox (no weights, no libraries, no network, no GPU) and the external compute profile required, with a CPU-only smoke-test option and its confounds. Do not report or imply any executed results or empirical outcome; the expected result remains unknown and should be labeled as such. Keep assumptions and uncertainties as explicit sections.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "4acfb260-f44b-44bf-bdc6-9734d46303c0", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:02:43.750346", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "c91db19c-a3f2-4285-bde8-59a9c5bf3df6", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved and is asking for the complete design document at stage=result. Everything is by design only; no results will be produced. Probably should write the complete design document into the response to end_researcher_turn. Should I also write to a file in /workspace for persistence? Writing the design document file could be a good deliverable. However, the deliverable is communicated through the response at handoff. I could also save a copy to /workspace/design.md — that's fine, and useful. Let me first create the document, write it to /workspace/DESIGN.md with execute_check, verify it, and then hand off at stage=result with the complete design in the response.\n\nLet me carefully draft the complete design.\n\nSections:\nA. Hypothesis and hypotheses to be tested\nB. Rationale linking conditions/controls to the hypothesis\nC. Protocol\n- Tasks: 8 CS primary + CommonsenseQA secondary. Task settings: harness names, metrics, sizes.\n  - boolq (3270 test), piqa (1000 validation? PIQA's test has no labels — harness uses validation), social_iqa (1000), hellaswag (10042), winogrande (1267), arc_easy (2376), arc_challenge (1172), openbookqa (500). CommonsenseQA validation set 1221.\n- Models: Llama-2-7b-chat-hf, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2, FuseLLM-7B official; re-fused checkpoint; α sweep.\n- Definition of fusion: θ_f(α) = α θ_Llama + (1−α) θ_Mistral, α=0.5; note that α=0 → pure Mistral, α=1 → pure Llama (identity negative control).\n- Chat templates: native ones; MC (multiple choice) format via harness's default templates (e.g., `multiple_choice` prompt format). Note that FuseLLM uses the Llama tokenizer (both parents share it).\n- Scoring: loglikelihood over candidate continuations; acc and acc_norm; primary: acc_norm for HellaSwag, OBQA, ARC (length bias) — researcher stated \"for length-biased tasks like HellaSwag/OBQA\"; need to justify the choice. Actually, to keep it simple: for the macro-average, define the primary per-task metric in advance: acc_norm for PIQA? The OpenLLM leaderboard uses acc for ARC and OBQA, and acc_norm for others... Let me define it as follows: primary metric = acc_norm for HellaSwag, PIQA, WinoGrande, SIQA, BoolQ? Hmm. Rationale: length normalization corrects for continuation-length bias, which matters most for HellaSwag and PIQA; OBQA targets have varying lengths. The researcher said \"for length-biased tasks like HellaSwag/OBQA... justify the choice rather than inheriting it.\" Let me state the rule explicitly: any task where the distribution of gold-choice length differs from the distractor lengths — empirically justifiable by computing mean choice length in the dataset (a dataset-side check, model-independent). Practical rule: primary = acc_norm for HellaSwag, PIQA, ARC-e, ARC-c, OBQA, WinoGrande (continuations are phrase-level with variable length); primary = acc for BoolQ (two fixed choices yes/no, equal length — acc and acc_norm are essentially the same modulo formatting), SIQA (choices of similar length); record both for all tasks. Macro-average computed on the per-task primary metric; secondary sensitivity analysis on the all-acc and all-acc_norm averages — good robustness check.\n- Decoding: none for the primary (likelihood scoring is deterministic). CommonsenseQA secondary: constrained generation with greedy temp=0 + exact match, plus likelihood scoring.\n- Batch/inference details: bf16, flash-attention optional (disabled or reported — determinism), sequence length cap, padding.\n- Hardware/compute budget estimate: total test items ≈ 8-task sum ≈ 3270+1000+1000+10042+1267+2376+1172+500 ≈ 20,627 instances; average ~2–4 candidate scorings each (HellaSwag is 4, others 2–4) → ~55k prompt-scoring forward passes, average ~200–400 tokens. One 7B forward pass on A100 is a few tokens/ms; rough estimate: one full 8-task zero-shot eval of one 7B model on a single A100-80GB ≈ 4–10 hours with batched loglikelihood; ~5 conditions (Llama, Mistral, official fusion, re-fusion, identity-check subset) + α sweep (5) → ~10 model-evals → ~2–4 GPU-days on 1×A100-80GB, or under a day on 4 GPUs data-parallel. CPU-only smoke test: 224 cores / 2TB RAM can hold bf16 weights but throughput is on the order of a few hundred tokens/sec; restrict to a fixed random subsample of ~200 items/task.\n\nD. List of controls (researcher-specified):\n1. Parent control (Mistral) — attribution analysis.\n2. Re-fusion control — reproduce the official checkpoint.\n3. Precision control — run one task (e.g., ARC-c) in bf16 and fp32 (and int8/GGUF) to estimate a quantization-precision artifact; quantization excluded from the headline.\n4. Tokenizer/architecture compatibility pre-check — both parents use the same Llama sentencepiece tokenizer (32000 vocab) and identical architecture config; verify layer-shape identity and weight-ordering check before interpolation; embedding-output-tie check.\n5. Identity negative controls α=1 and α=0 — the fused model built at α=1 must match Llama-2's accuracy exactly (perplexity diff = 0); α=0 must match Mistral. If there's a mismatch, the fusion implementation is broken.\n6. Scoring-metric sensitivity control (acc vs acc_norm).\n7. Random/permutation sanity check: e.g., sanity check that models are substantially above chance on each task (PIQA chance 50%, BoolQ chance via majority class ~62%, etc.) — implementation-error detector.\n8. Chat-template ablation (native template vs plain text) as a secondary check that template choice doesn't drive the conclusion.\n\nE. Analysis plan:\n- Pre-registered primary endpoint: Δ = mean(primary metric across the 8 tasks)(FuseLLM-7B official) − (Llama-2-7b-chat).\n- Inference: task-level item-wise paired bootstrap: for each task t, per-item correctness vector; bootstrap resample items within tasks with B=10,000 replicates, stratified by task (fixed task weights 1/8 each); compute Δ* per replicate → percentile 95% CI. Outperform claim requires CI lower bound > 0.\n- Sign/consistency check: report the number of tasks won (X/8); secondary Wilson CIs.\n- Multiple comparisons: per-task table is descriptive; if a confirmatory per-task claim is desired, Holm-Bonferroni across the 8 tasks; CommonsenseQA reported separately, excluded from multiplicity.\n- Secondary analyses: α-sweep trend test (isotonic/regression on accuracy vs α, test for interior peak at α≈0.5); parent attribution: Δ_fusion_vs_Llama vs (Mistral−Llama) — check that fusion gain isn't just Mistral's strength (e.g., fusion > max(parents)?) — note that \"exceeding both parents\" is a stronger claim; decomposition Δ_F−Δ_M.\n- Effect size: report Δ and per-task Cohen-style paired accuracy differences; macro-average bootstrap SE.\n- Precision: re-fusion agreement (mean absolute per-task difference between official vs re-fusion; expected to be within noise, e.g., <0.5pp) as pre-analysis validity gate.\n- Decision rule: if CI excludes 0, wins ≥6/8 tasks (sign test p<0.05 binomial against 0.5?), and the identity check passes, conclude \"under matched zero-shot, bf16 conditions, FuseLLM outperforms Llama-2 on the CS suite.\" If CI includes 0, \"no detectable difference\"; state that the outcome is unknown in advance.\n- Power/MD note: rough MDE: macro-avg SD unknown; item-level bootstrap on ~20k items gives task-level SEs of ~0.3–0.5pp per large task, macro-avg MDE ~1pp. Label as approximate.\n\nF. Feasibility:\n- Sandbox: cannot execute (no weights, no libraries, no network, no GPU). Design only.\n- External profile: 1×A100-80GB / 2×48GB, bf16 7B inference fits in ~15GB + activations; ~2–4 GPU-days full grid; 8×GPU for faster.\n- CPU smoke test: llama.cpp GGUF or HF accelerate device_map=cpu; confounds: quantization (required for CPU practicality if not bf16), very slow → subsample; not usable for headline.\n\nG. Assumptions section (researcher-specified decisions 1–7 restated as assumptions), H. Uncertainties section, I. Statement that no results have been run and expected outcome unknown.\n\nAlso mention versions: lm-eval-harness commit pinned (can't pin an exact version offline; state \"v0.4.x, commit to be pinned at execution\"), dataset revisions pinned, HF datasets with `revision`.\n\nAlso dataset details: PIQA is validation split (test unlabeled), SIQA validation (test private), CommonsenseQA validation (test labels private), WinoGrande validation (public test private label), HellaSwag is the harness's standard validation. Note as protocol detail: use the harness's default splits.\n\nCompute budget estimate: present the numbers.\n\nWrite the document to /workspace/DESIGN.md via a heredoc through execute_check. It might be long; that's fine. Then hand off the design (can be the same text) in the response. The response field should contain the complete design — yes, the researcher asked to \"include the complete design in the response.\" Include the full document in the response.\n\nLet me draft it concisely but completely. Keep it a reasonable size.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9fe026b6f01020e7", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9fe026b6f01020e7", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"mkdir -p /workspace && cat > /workspace/DESIGN.md << 'EOF'\\n# Experiment Design: Does FuseLLM outperform Llama-2 on commonsense (CS) benchmarks in zero-shot?\\n\\nStatus: DESIGN ONLY. No experiment has been executed; the empirical outcome is unknown and not implied anywhere in this document.\\n\\n## 0. Hypothesis and estimand\\nH1: Under matched zero-shot evaluation conditions, the fused model FuseLLM-7B (linear weight fusion of Llama-2-7b-chat and Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 at ratio alpha=0.5) achieves a higher macro-average accuracy over the 8-task Llama-2-lineage commonsense suite than Llama-2-7b-chat.\\nEstimand: Delta = mean_t[ Acc_t(FuseLLM) - Acc_t(Llama-2-7b-chat) ], t over the 8 primary CS tasks, primary per-task metric defined in Sec. 2.4, bf16, matched harness/prompts/splits.\\n\\n## 1. Conditions (each rationale tied to H1)\\n| # | Condition | Role | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| C1 | Llama-2-7b-chat (hf) | Hypothesis target / baseline | The model named in the question; all deltas are defined against it. |\\n| C2 | FuseLLM-7B (official release) | Primary test condition | The exact artifact the question asks about. |\\n| C3 | Local re-fusion, alpha=0.5 (theta_f = 0.5*theta_Llama + 0.5*theta_Mistral) | Reproducibility control | Verifies the official checkpoint is reconstructible by the declared fusion recipe; agreement gate, not the headline number. |\\n| C4 | Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 | Parent control | Attributes delta to fusion rather than to a stronger parent; enables 'fusion > max(parents)' decomposition. |\\n| C5 | alpha sweep {0.0, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 1.0} (local fusion) | Diagnostic/secondary | alpha=0/1 are identity negative controls (Sec. 3); interior peak vs monotone trend tests whether '0.5 fusion' is meaningful vs a lucky point. |\\n| C6 | CommonsenseQA (secondary task, C1/C2/C4 only) | Scope coverage | Question phrasing 'CommenSense(CS)' maps to the 8-task Llama-2 CS set; CommonsenseQA is reported separately so both readings are covered without contaminating the primary metric. |\\n\\n## 2. Protocol\\n### 2.1 Tasks (primary suite, harness identifiers, splits, N)\\nBoolQ (boolq, test, 3270); PIQA (piqa, validation, 1000; test unlabeled); SocialIQA (social_iqa, validation, 1000; test private); HellaSwag (hellaswag, validation, 10042); WinoGrande (winogrande, validation, 1267; public test labels private); ARC-Easy (arc_easy, test, 2376); ARC-Challenge (arc_challenge, test, 1172); OpenBookQA (openbookqa, test, 500). Secondary: CommonsenseQA (commonsense_qa, validation, 1221; official test labels private). Dataset revisions pinned by commit hash at run time; same pinned revisions for every condition.\\n\\n### 2.2 Harness and zero-shot setting\\nEleutherAI lm-evaluation-harness, v0.4.x (exact commit pinned in the run manifest), task configs unmodified except prompt_type as in 2.3, num_fewshot=0 for all tasks and conditions. No task-specific prompt engineering; prompts depend only on condition through that condition's native chat template.\\n\\n### 2.3 Prompting\\nEach condition is served with its own native chat template (Llama-2 chat template for C1/C5-family, Mistral template for C4, and the template shipped with the official FuseLLM config for C2/C3; C3/C5 use the Llama template since fusion is in Llama-2 architecture space and both parents share the Llama tokenizer). Task framing uses harness-default zero-shot templates, identical across conditions. Secondary template ablation: plain-text (no chat template) for C1/C2 on a fixed subsample, to verify the verdict is not template-driven.\\n\\n### 2.4 Scoring and primary metric\\nAll MC tasks scored by padded log-likelihood of each answer continuation given the prompt (no generation); both acc (unnormalized) and acc_norm (length-normalized) recorded for every task.\\nPre-registered primary per-task metric: acc_norm for tasks whose candidate continuations are free-form and length-heterogeneous (HellaSwag, PIQA, SIQA, WinoGrande, ARC-e, ARC-c, OBQA); acc for BoolQ (two fixed, near-equal-length yes/no options; normalization only adds format noise). Justification: length normalization removes the continuation-length bias that inflates/deflates scores on HellaSwag-class and OBQA-style items; the choice is made from dataset continuation-length statistics (computable without models) rather than inherited from leaderboard conventions. Secondary sensitivity analysis recomputes the whole analysis with all-acc and with all-acc_norm; the verdict must be reported against both if it flips.\\nCommonsenseQA (secon