{"id": "02ed4353-1b03-42fc-9799-e167b5593cc0", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:04:42.711760", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a62b2ed8-19fa-4829-96b4-51d3668d90f8", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Part 1 written. Now appending the remaining sections:"}], "reasoning_content": "Let's have you continue with Part 2.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat >> /workspace/DESIGN.md << 'EOF'\n\n## 4. Analysis plan (pre-registered)\nPrimary endpoint: Delta as defined in Sec. 0.\nInference: item-level paired bootstrap, B=10,000. For each replicate: resample items with replacement within each task (tasks fixed at equal weight 1/8), reuse the SAME resampled item indices across conditions (paired), compute per-task accuracy differences on the primary metric, average across tasks -> Delta*. Report percentile 95% CI.\nConsistency check: per-task win count X/8 (ties excluded from direction counts); binomial sign test reported descriptively. Per-task table: Holm-Bonferroni adjusted p-values from per-task paired item bootstraps ONLY if per-task claims are made; otherwise the table is descriptive (researcher directive: per-task wins are a descriptor, not a gate). CommonsenseQA sits outside primary multiplicity handling.\nDecision rule (fixed in advance): (a) conclude 'FuseLLM outperforms Llama-2 on CS (zero-shot)' iff lower bound of the 95% CI for Delta > 0 AND identity/compatibility/re-fusion/precision controls pass; (b) 'no detectable difference' if the CI covers 0; (c) 'verdict withheld / implementation ambiguity' if any gate fails. A negative direction is reported as outperformance failure. All three states are legitimate outcomes; no expected direction is asserted.\nSecondary: alpha-sweep curve with per-point CIs and peak-vs-monotone check; parent attribution decomposition (is fusion > max(parents)?); CommonsenseQA in its own table; template ablation and acc/acc_norm sensitivity annexes.\nApproximate sensitivity (design-time estimate, not a data-driven power study): with ~20.6k paired items and strong item correlation, macro-average bootstrap SE is expected on the order of ~0.3-0.8 pp; minimum resolvable Delta roughly ~1 pp. Smaller true deltas map to outcome (b) by design.\nVariance handling: no sampling seeds drive the primary endpoint (deterministic likelihood scoring); residual nondeterminism is bounded by the Sec. 2.6 batch-order check and enters the C3 agreement gate, not H1 inference.\n\n## 5. Feasibility\nVerified sandbox state (inspection only; nothing model-related was executed): /workspace contains only task.json; no FuseLLM/Llama code, configs or weights anywhere on disk; no torch/transformers/lm-eval/numpy installed; network disabled (DNS resolution failure confirmed); no GPU (nvidia-smi fails); 224 CPU cores, ~2 TB RAM. Therefore the experiment CANNOT be executed in this sandbox - design-only deliverable, per task note.\nExternal profile required: 1x 80 GB GPU (A100/H100) or 2x >=48 GB; Linux; pinned lm-evaluation-harness commit and pinned dataset revisions; HF gated access for meta-llama/Llama-2-7b-chat-hf, mistralai/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2, fusellm/FuseLLM-7B; ~50 GB disk for checkpoints; budget per Sec. 2.7 (~2-4 GPU-days on 1x A100-80GB).\nCPU-only smoke-test option (NOT for headline results): GGUF-quantized or device_map=cpu bf16 inference on the 224-core / 2 TB host with a fixed subsample of 200 items/task, purpose limited to pipeline debugging (template rendering, scoring plumbing, output parsing). Declared confounds: quantization shifts scores by an amount only bounded by control 3.3; per-task CI at n=200 is roughly +/-4-8 pp; full-suite CPU bf16 scoring would take weeks. Smoke results must never enter the decision rule.\n\n## 6. Assumptions (researcher-directed design choices A1-A7, recorded as stated choices, not verified paper facts)\nA1. Primary CS suite = Llama-2-lineage 8 tasks (BoolQ, PIQA, SIQA, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, ARC-e, ARC-c, OBQA); CommonsenseQA secondary, reported separately.\nA2. 'Outperform' = macro-average accuracy difference FuseLLM(alpha=0.5) minus Llama-2-7b-chat under matched zero-shot conditions; item-level paired bootstrap 95% CI is the gate; per-task record is a descriptor.\nA3. Official FuseLLM-7B checkpoint is the primary fused condition; local linear re-fusion at the same alpha is a reproducibility control; alpha sweep is diagnostic/secondary.\nA4. Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 is evaluated under the identical protocol as parent-attribution control.\nA5. Native chat templates + harness-default zero-shot task prompts, identical across conditions; both acc and acc_norm recorded; acc_norm primary for length-biased tasks, justified from dataset continuation-length statistics, not inherited leaderboard settings.\nA6. bf16 for the primary analysis; explicit quantization-confound control; no quantized numbers in the headline comparison.\nA7. Published FuseLLM/Llama-2 accuracies are reference context only; the paper's leaderboard-style settings may not be zero-shot, so absolute values are expected to differ; the decision rule is purely internal and matched.\n\n## 7. Uncertainties\nU1. Source paper setup sections (iclr2024/18013/1:setup_2, setup_9) could not be read (no network); suite membership, fusion-ratio convention, shot counts and prompt templates in the paper remain unverified against this design, which follows researcher directives A1-A7 instead.\nU2. Chat-template vs raw-text prompting shifts absolute accuracies; the template ablation limits but does not eliminate this uncertainty for the direction of the headline delta.\nU3. The per-task acc vs acc_norm choice is a judgment call; sensitivity annex bounds its impact.\nU4. Provenance of the official FuseLLM-7B checkpoint (exact parent revisions/alpha) is unverified; control 3.2 detects gross mismatch but a closely-related different parent revision could still pass.\nU5. Gated-asset access and exact harness-revision replication are external execution logistics, not design-logic risks.\nU6. Approximate MDE of ~1 pp means genuinely small true deltas resolve to 'no detectable difference' by design, not by evidence of equivalence.\n\n## 8. Outcome statement\nNo experiment has been run and no result is claimed. Whether H1 holds is UNKNOWN; the pre-registered decision rule specifies how each possible outcome would be labeled.\nEOF\necho done; wc -c /workspace/DESIGN.md; grep -c '^##' /workspace/DESIGN.md", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bf764fd53a5bebfa", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-bf764fd53a5bebfa", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat >> /workspace/DESIGN.md << 'EOF'\\n\\n## 4. Analysis plan (pre-registered)\\nPrimary endpoint: Delta as defined in Sec. 0.\\nInference: item-level paired bootstrap, B=10,000. For each replicate: resample items with replacement within each task (tasks fixed at equal weight 1/8), reuse the SAME resampled item indices across conditions (paired), compute per-task accuracy differences on the primary metric, average across tasks -> Delta*. Report percentile 95% CI.\\nConsistency check: per-task win count X/8 (ties excluded from direction counts); binomial sign test reported descriptively. Per-task table: Holm-Bonferroni adjusted p-values from per-task paired item bootstraps ONLY if per-task claims are made; otherwise the table is descriptive (researcher directive: per-task wins are a descriptor, not a gate). CommonsenseQA sits outside primary multiplicity handling.\\nDecision rule (fixed in advance): (a) conclude 'FuseLLM outperforms Llama-2 on CS (zero-shot)' iff lower bound of the 95% CI for Delta > 0 AND identity/compatibility/re-fusion/precision controls pass; (b) 'no detectable difference' if the CI covers 0; (c) 'verdict withheld / implementation ambiguity' if any gate fails. A negative direction is reported as outperformance fai