{"id": "190684de-48b8-49b2-8572-276f3932df43", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:06:24.956151", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "f1211faf-56b3-4cc3-b5a4-e64d61c9dc19", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The complete design document is finalized and saved at `/workspace/DESIGN.md`. Handing off the full design."}], "reasoning_content": "The document was written to /workspace/DESIGN.md (13.7 KB, sections present — 16 matches for '^##' including subsections). Next, I'll hand off at stage=result and include the full design in the answer. The answer needs to include the complete design. I'll reproduce the content of the document (abridged but complete). Include the full document text in the answer, and cite the file as evidence.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# FINAL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only; nothing executed; outcome UNKNOWN)\nFull copy saved at /workspace/DESIGN.md. Researcher directives A1–A7 (§6) are recorded as stated design choices, not paper facts.\n\n## 0. Hypothesis and estimand\nH1: Under matched zero-shot conditions, FuseLLM-7B (linear weight fusion of Llama-2-7b-chat + Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 at α=0.5) achieves higher macro-average accuracy on the 8-task Llama-2-lineage commonsense (CS) suite than Llama-2-7b-chat.\nEstimand: Δ = mean_t[Acc_t(FuseLLM) − Acc_t(Llama-2-7b-chat)], t over 8 primary tasks, primary per-task metric per §2.4, bf16, matched harness/prompts/splits.\n\n## 1. Conditions and rationale\n- C1 Llama-2-7b-chat — hypothesis target; all deltas defined against it.\n- C2 FuseLLM-7B official checkpoint — primary test condition (the artifact in the question).\n- C3 Local re-fusion θ_f = 0.5θ_Llama + 0.5θ_Mistral — reproducibility control (agreement gate, not headline).\n- C4 Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 — parent control; attributes Δ to fusion rather than a stronger parent; enables 'fusion > max(parents)' decomposition.\n- C5 α-sweep {0.0,0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9,1.0} — diagnostic/secondary; α=0/1 double as identity negative controls; peak-vs-monotone trend tests whether α=0.5 is meaningful.\n- C6 CommonsenseQA (secondary task; C1/C2/C4 only) — covers the alternate reading of \"CommenSense\" without contaminating the primary average.\n\n## 2. Protocol\n2.1 Tasks (harness id, split, N): boolq(test,3270), piqa(val,1000), social_iqa(val,1000), hellaswag(val,10042), winogrande(val,1267), arc_easy(test,2376), arc_challenge(test,1172), openbookqa(test,500); secondary commonsense_qa(val,1221). Splits chosen because public test labels are unavailable for PIQA/SIQA/WinoGrande/CSQA (harness defaults). Dataset revisions pinned by commit; identical for all conditions.\n2.2 Harness: EleutherAI lm-evaluation-harness v0.4.x (commit pinned in run manifest); num_fewshot=0 everywhere; no task-specific prompt engineering.\n2.3 Prompting: each condition uses its native chat template (Llama template for C1/C3/C5; Mistral template for C4; template shipped with official FuseLLM config for C2); harness-default zero-shot task framing, identical across conditions. Secondary template ablation (plain text, C1/C2, fixed subsample).\n2.4 Scoring: padded log-likelihood over answer continuations (no generation in the primary protocol); record both acc and acc_norm for every task. Pre-registered primary metric: acc_norm for length-heterogeneous continuation tasks (HellaSwag, PIQA, SIQA, WinoGrande, ARC-e, ARC-c, OBQA); acc for BoolQ (two equal-length fixed options). Justification from dataset continuation-length statistics (model-free computation), not leaderboard inheritance. Sensitivity annex reruns everything all-acc and all-acc_norm; if the verdict flips it must be reported. CommonsenseQA: likelihood over options + greedy (temp 0) exact match, descriptive only. No sampling anywhere → no seed-driven variance in the primary endpoint.\n2.5 Fusion: elementwise interpolation of correspondingly NAMED weight tensors; no retraining/scale-matching; requires §3.4 pre-check; α=0.5 primary; official C2 never assumed equal to local fusion (C3 measures the gap).\n2.6 Inference: bf16; padding_side='left'; flash-attention off for determinism; reproducibility check = C2 on ARC-c × 3 batch sizes, per-item logprob agreement ≤1e-3, spread folded into the C3 gate.\n2.7 Compute budget (estimate, not measurement): ~20,627 items × ~2–4 candidate scorings ≈ 55k short forward passes; ≈4–10 GPU-h per 7B full 8-task zero-shot eval on 1×A100-80GB; full grid + sweep + controls ≈ 2–4 GPU-days on 1×A100-80GB, <1 day on 4×.\n\n## 3. Controls (pre-registered)\n3.1 Parent control (C4): report Δ(F−L), Δ(M−L), and fusion-vs-max(parents).\n3.2 Re-fusion control (C3 vs C2): gate = mean per-task |diff| ≤ 0.5 pp and same decision direction; failure reported as checkpoint/recipe ambiguity; headline stays on official C2.\n3.3 Precision/quantization control: ARC-c + WinoGrande for C1/C2 at bf16 vs fp32 (plus one int8/GGUF pass feeding the CPU smoke path); any claimed Δ smaller than observed bf16–fp32 drift is declared precision-confounded; quantized numbers excluded from headline.\n3.4 Tokenizer/architecture compatibility pre-check (model-free): identical tokenizer (Llama sentencepiece/vocab) and identical tensor names/shapes before interpolation; load by names, never positional indices; mismatch aborts C3/C5.\n3.5 Identity negative controls: α=1.0 must reproduce C1 within §2.6 spread; α=0.0 must reproduce C4; failure invalidates the fusion pipeline. Above-chance sanity checks per task/model (PIQA≥50%, ARC≥25%, BoolQ vs majority class) catch broken scoring plumbing.\n3.6 Template ablation; 3.7 acc/acc_norm metric-family sensitivity.\n\n## 4. Analysis plan (pre-registered)\nPrimary endpoint Δ(§0). Inference: item-level paired bootstrap, B=10,000 — resample items with replacement within each task, SAME indices reused across conditions (paired), tasks fixed at weight 1/8; percentile 95% CI on Δ. Consistency: per-task win count X/8 with descriptive binomial sign test. Per-task table: Holm–Bonferroni adjusted per-task bootstrap p-values only if per-task claims are made; otherwise descriptive (per-task wins are a descriptor, not a gate, per directive A2). CommonsenseQA outside primary multiplicity handling.\nDecision rule (fixed in advance): (a) \"FuseLLM outperforms Llama-2 on CS (zero-shot)\" iff 95% CI lower bound for Δ > 0 AND all gates (3.2, 3.4, 3.5, precision drift) pass; (b) \"no detectable difference\" if CI covers 0; (c) \"verdict withheld / implementation ambiguity\" if any gate fails; negative direction = outperformance failure. All states legitimate; no expected direction asserted.\nSecondary: α-sweep curve with per-point CIs and peak-vs-monotone check; parent attribution; CommonsenseQA table; template and metric-family annexes. Approximate sensitivity (design-time, not a power study): ~20.6k paired items → macro-avg bootstrap SE ~0.3–0.8 pp, minimum resolvable Δ ≈ 1 pp; smaller true deltas map to (b) by design.\n\n## 5. Feasibility\nVerified sandbox state (inspection only): /workspace = task.json only; no FuseLLM/Llama code, weights, or eval harness anywhere on disk; no torch/transformers/lm-eval/numpy; network disabled (DNS failure confirmed); no GPU; 224 CPU cores / ~2 TB RAM. The experiment CANNOT run here — design-only per the task note. External requirement: 1×80 GB GPU (A100/H100) or 2×≥48 GB; pinned harness commit + dataset revisions; gated HF access to Llama-2-7b-chat-hf, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2, FuseLLM-7B; ~50 GB disk; ~2–4 GPU-days. CPU-only smoke test (never headline): GGUF or device_map=cpu, fixed 200 items/task, pipeline debugging only; declared confounds: quantization drift (bounded only by 3.3), ±4–8 pp per-task CI at n=200, weeks for full-suite CPU bf16.\n\n## 6. Assumptions (= researcher directives A1–A7, stated choices, not verified paper facts)\nA1 8-task Llama-2 CS suite primary; CommonsenseQA secondary/separate. A2 'Outperform' = macro-avg accuracy Δ (FuseLLM α=0.5 − Llama-2-7b-chat), paired-bootstrap 95% CI gate, per-task = descriptor. A3 Official FuseLLM-7B primary; local re-fusion = reproducibility control; α-sweep diagnostic. A4 Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2 under identical protocol as parent control. A5 Native chat templates + harness-default zero-shot prompts; acc and acc_norm recorded; acc_norm primary for length-biased tasks with dataset-statistics justification. A6 bf16 primary + explicit quantization control; no quantized headline numbers. A7 Published paper numbers are reference context only (paper leaderboard-style settings may not be zero-shot; absolute values expected to differ); decision rule is internal/matched.\n\n## 7. Uncertainties\nU1 Paper setup sections (iclr2024/18013/1:setup_2, setup_9) unreadable offline — design follows A1–A7, not verified paper text. U2 Chat-template effects on absolute scores (mitigated, not eliminated, by template ablation). U3 acc-vs-acc_norm per-task choice is a judgment bounded by sensitivity annex. U4 Official checkpoint provenance unverified (3.2 catches gross mismatch only). U5 Gated-asset access / harness revision logistics. U6 ~1 pp MDE: small true deltas resolve to \"no detectable difference\" by design, not equivalence evidence.\n\n## 8. Outcome statement\nNo experiment was run; no result is claimed or implied. Whether H1 holds is UNKNOWN; the pre-registered decision rule fixes how each possible outcome will be labeled.", "evidence": "REPOSITORY/ENVIRONMENT OBSERVATIONS (all verified this session; no results fabricated): (1) /workspace contains only task.json; filesystem-wide find for fusellm/llama/commonsense/hellaswag/winogrande/lm_eval/*.whl returned nothing; no /data, /models, /opt, no HF cache. (2) Python 3.14.4; pip list shows zero ML/eval dependencies (no torch, transformers, datasets, accelerate, lm-eval, numpy, scipy). (3) Network disabled — urllib to pypi.org failed with 'Temporary failure in name resolution'. (4) nvidia-smi fails (no GPU); 224 cores; ~2 TB RAM — sufficient to host 7B we